{
  "id": "COMPOUND:33489 - PROTEIN:6323",
  "verbalization": "Pseudacyclin A appears to have a shared target, CCRF-HSB-2, which is also a target of multiple compounds, including 19(E)-18-demethoxygardfloramine and Trigoneoside IB. These compounds all direct their activity to DNA polymerase iota, suggesting a potential mechanism of action involving the regulation of DNA polymerase activity. The evidence indicates that Pseudacyclin A may exert its effects through a pathway that involves the modulation of CCRF-HSB-2, which in turn influences DNA polymerase iota. The high scores in all three paths suggest a consistent and significant relationship, though the exact mechanism by which Pseudacyclin A interacts with CCRF-HSB-2 to affect DNA polymerase iota remains to be fully elucidated. The presence of multiple targets and their shared downstream effect on DNA polymerase iota supports the hypothesis that Pseudacyclin A may act through a pathway that involves the regulation of DNA polymerase activity, possibly through the modulation of its target, CCRF-HSB-2.",
